The full binding vocabulary, verified:

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-child',
  template: `
    {{ title }}                          <!-- 1. interpolation: class → DOM text -->
    <img [src]="avatarUrl" [alt]="title">  <!-- 2. property: class → DOM property -->
    <button (click)="save()">Save</button> <!-- 3. event: DOM → class method -->
    <input [(ngModel)]="name">            <!-- 4. two-way: banana-in-a-box -->
  `,
})
export class Child {
  @Input() title = '';                   // parent → child: property binding on the tag
  @Output() saved = new EventEmitter<string>(); // child → parent: event binding on the tag
  save() { this.saved.emit('done'); }    // parent receives via (saved)="onSaved($event)"
}
```

The mental model (the interview core): data flows ONE WAY by default — down through @Input, up through @Output events. Two-way [(ngModel)] is sugar over both: [ngModel]="name" sets, (ngModelChange)="name = $event" updates — the box-in-bananas order [( )] is literally [ ] inside ( ), which is exactly what it desugars to.

The three traps worth naming:
1. PROPERTY vs ATTRIBUTE — [src] binds the DOM PROPERTY (current value, updates live); [attr.src] writes the HTML attribute (static, string-only). Properties are what you want 95% of the time; attributes matter for ARIA and SVG where no property exists.
2. INPUT MUTATION — a child mutating an @Input object changes the object the PARENT also holds. With OnPush parents, mutation isn't detected (Object.is sees the same reference). The fix: children treat inputs as read-only; parents pass new references.
3. TEMPLATE REFERENCE — #var on an element hands the template the DOM node (or directive instance like #f="ngForm`) — the bridge for reading form state without a round-trip through the class.
